es and cared  about them and in which the state took  serious responsibility for economic  performance and well-being global  capitalism today really has no loyalty  to people or place at all global  corporations too often exist beyond the  reach of of tax authorities and  regulators government's claim you know  who claim that they believe markets will  solve everything wash their hands of  these affairs and it's not surprising  that people feel insecure on the left  however democracy really matters it  doesn't on the right where the voices of  people of the people know all people  have nothing to fear from the populism  if you want to call it that of the left  they certainly have plenty to fear from  the populism of Trump and Faraj and of  from the far right which is now growing  is a serious threat throughout Europe  and the Western world  john lunsford thank you very clear  statement there let me turn straight to  Michael Howard  well the overarching title of this  session is the will of the people and  the will of the people must prevail if  you believe in democracy there can't be  any other answer than that whether  populism and I don't think it's either a  right or a left wing thing and I think  it is a thing incidentally whether  whether populism is to be welcomed or  feared depends exactly on what you mean  by populism because it means all sort of  things all sorts of people there's a  reference in our brief maybe in what  you've read to ideology populism is  different from ideology they're not the  same things and I'll be bold enough to  offer you a definition which is that  populism is an upsurge of resentment  against an elite that is perceived often  correctly to be out of touch and out of  sympathy with the anxieties and the  concerns and the frustrations of those  people who are resentful and if that's a  if that's a reasonable definition then I  don't think it's either to be welcomed  to ought to be feared I think it's the  duty of politicians to engage with it to  recognize that it exists and to try to  engage with it and engaging with it  means addressing the concerns that have  led to that anxiety and frustration what  do I mean by addressing those concerns  sometimes you can actually meet them  sometimes you can identify the grievance  and you can actually take policy  measures which will which will  ameliorate the concerns that have led to  that grievance sometimes it's much more  difficult and it's either impossible or  actually undesirable to meet those  grievances in which case your duty I  think as a politician is to to explain  and to persuade and to persuade exactly  why it is that that grievance can't  actually be  met and explained why perhaps it's a  grievance without foundation and the  concern underlying it is misplaced and  and the policy which is being complained  about actually works to the benefit of  the people who are complaining that's  obviously not an easy thing to do but it  is part of the leadership of politicians  which is so necessary if populism is not  to boil over into the kind of anger  which feeds extremes and I take a dim  view of extremes of the right and of the  left and I think one of the reasons why  I am so keen that politicians should  engage with the concerns of populism is  in order to ensure that neither the  extremes of the right nor the extremes  of the left again a a foothold in power  of course it's not simply a concern in  this country it's not simply an issue or  a thing in this country it is something  which is apparent pretty well across the  Western world and it's only in my view  by engaging with it that leaders will  prevent the anger from boiling over and  we'll make populism a force for good as  part of the overall proposition that the  will of the people must prevail for more  debates talks and interviews subscribe  today to the institutes of arson ideas  III TV  you

